{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/pulls/2257","id":5319890972,"node_id":"PR_kwDOTIDrTc8AAAABPRcYHA","html_url":"https://github.com/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/pull/2257","diff_url":"https://github.com/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/pull/2257.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/pull/2257.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/issues/2257","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/pulls/2257/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/pulls/2257/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/issues/2257/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/CorbanuCore/CorbanuTerminal/statuses/16ff8474665e292f84305be2c18c58c678b73008","number":2257,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"fix: enforce remote sandbox refusal for discovery and skill reads","user":{"login":"IridiumMaster","id":4607679,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ2MDc2Nzk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4607679?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster","html_url":"https://github.com/IridiumMaster","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IridiumMaster/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Refs #2088; follows #2087 (D510/D491).\n\nModerate and Aggressive now carry the existing sandbox refusal through executor skill/plugin discovery and resource reads, including full-disk profiles and catalogs captured before a live security-level change. Remote discovery checks the executor before sending roots, and the receiving executor rechecks elevated sandbox setup for marked file operations. Ready remote environments without a usable sandbox receive a startup notice with setup steps. Permissive behavior and the wire schema are unchanged.\n\nThis is a bounded repair of D491/D510. Product citation: **Reconciled security scope — TO BUILD**, “preserves Permissive compatibility and makes the broker mandatory for both Moderate and Aggressive.” Release-note fragment and sandbox documentation included.\n\nValidation on glitch, Rust 1.99.1, isolated test homes and target:\n- Four regressions fail on unchanged main: discovery precheck, full-disk context, live skill-read context, and startup notice. All pass after the fix using the existing harness/fake executor.\n- Focused exec-server tests: 6 passed. Full skills-extension suite: 72 passed. Focused core tests: 68 passed. Integration regressions (#1976/#2000/#2050/#2167, including the #2088 startup assertions): 20 passed. Developer-accounting #2088 regression: 1 passed.\n- `just fix` made no changes; clippy including tests with `-D warnings` passed. Matching local worktree `just fmt`, diff checks, and release-fragment validation passed.\n- An initial pass-after run reused a stale baseline skills library because archive synchronization preserved old timestamps. Refreshed changed-source timestamps and reran fix, clippy and all listed tests successfully. No tests were removed. Native Windows elevated-not-set-up fail-before and a real Windows remote executor/TUI exercise were not run locally. Native Windows CI, including clippy, tests, integration, security probes, and credential canaries, passed.\n\nNative Windows clippy exposed a Send-proof recursion overflow through the new startup await. Boxing that future bounds the compiler's type proof without changing behavior or tests. Core fix/clippy, 68 focused tests, 20 integration tests, and developer-accounting all pass again on the revised head.\n\nIndependent read-only Opus 5.5 High review round 3: **APPROVE** on `16ff8474665e292f84305be2c18c58c678b73008` (3/5 rounds). Matching corbanu-security approval posted after that verdict. All 42 applicable CI checks passed (10 workflow skips, no failures or pending checks). SAME-TEST, clean merged-tree/blob-size, exact-head approval, and literal-head merge guards passed. Merged as `66da17e93cff18fde7ca412e7a500f9747c50d70`.\n\nCompatibility limits: protected discovery requires the existing discoverV1 capability (introduced before hostSandbox reporting); older executors need updating. Startup notices cover connections ready at startup and check them sequentially; deferred connections explain refusal on first use.\n\nRemaining #2088 decisions for Travis:\n- Items 1/6: add a negotiated launch requirement and actual per-process confinement provenance, or retain client prechecks. Recommend a separately signed-off, backward-compatible protocol design preserving unmarked Permissive launches. The existing-field file-operation case is fixed here.\n- Item 3: correct PF-27-S06's target-platform check across all levels, or restrict it to protected modes. Recommend the complete correction after explicit security sign-off because it also changes post-taint Permissive behavior.\n- Item 5: cache positive sandbox reports by URL, or keep fresh checks until executor identity/invalidation is specified. Recommend fresh checks; a time-only cache weakens refusal after an executor swap or setup change.\n","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-11-15T05:40:22Z","updated_at":"2026-11-15T07:00:31Z","closed_at"